 "capsule": "Centralist Mexico denotes the unitary regime instituted by the December 1836 Siete Leyes, which abolished the 1824 federal system and reorganized the states into departments run by centrally appointed governors, later continued under the 1843 Bases Orgánicas. This decade of chronic coups and secessions—including the loss of Texas (1836) and Yucatán rebellions—also encompassed the Pastry War (1838) and the ruinous Mexican-American War (1846–48); federalism and the 1824 constitution were formally restored on 22 August 1846. This unitary-republic phase is distinct from the later consolidated federal 'Mexico' sharing this identifier.",
